hi there steve kaufman here the linguist  on language  uh today i want to talk about  teaching credentials particularly  language teaching credentials  we have  tessel teaching english as a second  language  and other credentials that  many many people  study for and acquire  and these are very useful in terms of  getting jobs as teachers  but my interest is  how important is it from the perspective  of the learner how important is the  teacher's credential  in determining whether the teacher is  effective  my belief is that the teachers  credentials are not important at all  however  it's certainly the case that the  teachers  place great value on these credentials  and whenever  the results of teaching for example  language teaching  say in the case of uh  immigrant language teaching in canada i  was speaking to the the government  department in the immigration department  that looks after teaching of immigrants  and they were saying that  that they haven't they aren't having  much success and one of the things  they're going to do is raise the  credential standards and  more money on training and so forth and  this is inevitably what teachers like to  do they would like to see more money  spent on training  more money spent on creating more jobs  but from the perspective of the learner  i don't think the credentials matter at  all  and i was reading a book on my kindle  called liberating learning  technology politics and the future of  american education written by two  academics terry moe moe and john chubb  and one of the interesting comments in  there was  that a variety of of different uh you  know research  has shown that credentials have very  little bearing  on  the results in education generally and  i'm sure language education is no  exception  uh what seems to matter the most is the  verbal ability of a teacher the ability  of the teacher to speak well in an  interesting way engage the students  that's the number one thing  the second thing is the competence  that the teacher has in the subject  and the third thing is the experience  of the teacher  so if we apply that to language teaching  uh obviously the ability to speak the  native language is extremely important  and even if it's a non-native speaker  the ability to speak the language is  extremely important the second thing is  the competence  and here i think we're talking about  a and ability to speak the language well  and second of all as a language teacher  the experience of having learned  one or more foreign languages  and third of all in so far as experience  i hear again it's the experience of  teaching which i think is extremely  important more important than any  credentials  and second of all again the experience  of having learned a language so i think  all of these things are more important  now is the teacher important period well  i think that the teacher can be  extremely important even in my own case  even though i am very much an  independent learner and i think people  should be independent learners and the  best language learners are independent  learners  somewhere somebody has to turn that  switch on now for some lucky people  they just as youngsters get very  interested in other cultures and other  languages but most people are at some  point influenced by someone else  that someone else can be a teacher  and in my own case at the age of 17 at  mcgill university it was a french  professor who turned me on to french  culture and the french language and that  made me an independent learner initially  of french and then of other languages  so i think the teacher can be a  tremendous influence the teacher is an  influence in terms of  motivating the learner and so there we  get to the sort of verbal skills  communication skills of the teacher  competence and therefore confidence in  teaching the language and of course  experience as a teacher and as a learner  all of these things  make it more likely that that teacher  will be a positive  influence on the learner turn on the  learner  credentials  learning how to theoretically how one  should teach modal verbs and when i mean  that's to my mind irrelevant there are  textbooks you can either follow the  textbook or not follow the textbook it's  my experience that any textbook i have  seen that seeks to teach  certain parts of speech in certain order  and expects the learner to learn them  are doomed to failure anyway because the  learner is going to learn them on their  own timetable and as i've said before  learn primarily through massive input i  mean we've built the link system around  the idea that if you listen a lot if you  read a lot if you save and review a lot  of words and phrases you will gradually  get used to the language and you can at  your own speed  review some grammar points in a small  book uh googling on the internet or in a  grammar class it doesn't really matter  but it's not crucial it's not essential  to your learning of the language and  what the teachers tend to do  they  reinforce one of the most unfortunate  habits and attitudes of learners and  that is the feeling that they have to  nail things down they have to learn it  chapter one i have to learn chapter one  for my teacher for my test i have to  learn chapter two if i can't remember  the words if i can't remember the  grammar then there's something wrong  with me it's pressure it's all of that  is very unpleasant and it comes from the  teachers and that's what they're taught  to do  in terms of getting their  their certificates  i i should say i've never been to one of  those courses but i assume that that's  what they are taught to do because  that's what they do when you go to their  classes so  in summary then i think that credentials  are not important i think there's a  very unhealthy insistence on credentials  throughout the education system  i think that the analysis and and  basically the conclusion of these two  authors chub and mo  based on research  that it's more a matter of verbal  ability ability to communicate  competence in the subject and of course  personality plus some experience these  are the things that make successful  teachers successful teachers turn on  learners they don't insist that learners  do this for this test learn these  chapters because i taught it this  semester  all of those kinds of things and  many of the explanations that i get from  the experienced credentialed teachers  are unwelcome i don't want them what i  want is an engaging person that's going  to turn me onto the language so  not surprisingly i'm not a fan of  credentials and i think credentials are  only one part of the sort of rigid  structure of of language education  which is the reason why so many people  are turned off language learning  so i look forward to  your comments thank you
 

